<DIV align="left" style="font-size: 10pt;color: #000000; background: #ffffff; margin-top: 6pt; margin-left: 0; margin-right: 0; margin-bottom: 0; ">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
We could suffer a loss due to the cost to repair any damage to
properties that are not insured or are underinsured. There are
types of losses, generally of a catastrophic nature, such as
losses due to terrorism, wars, earthquakes or acts of God, that
are either uninsurable or not economically insurable. We may
acquire properties that are located in areas where there exists
a risk of hurricanes, earthquakes, floods or other acts of God.
Generally, we will not obtain insurance for hurricanes,
earthquakes, floods or other acts of God unless required by a
lender or our advisor determines that such insurance is
necessary and may be obtained on a cost-effective basis. If such
a catastrophic event were to occur, or cause the destruction of
one or more of our properties, we could lose both our invested
capital and anticipated profits from such property.
</DIV>

