<!-- link1 "Taxation of Taxable U.S. Stockholders" -->

<DIV align="left" style="font-size: 10pt;color: #000000; background: #ffffff; margin-top: 12pt; margin-left: 0; margin-right: 0; margin-bottom: 0; ">
<B>Taxation of Taxable U.S.&nbsp;Stockholders</B>
</DIV>

<DIV align="left" style="font-size: 10pt;color: #000000; background: #ffffff; margin-top: 6pt; margin-left: 0; margin-right: 0; margin-bottom: 0; ">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
As long as we qualify as a REIT, a taxable
&#147;U.S.&nbsp;stockholder&#148; must take into account, as
ordinary income, distributions out of our current or accumulated
earnings and profits and that we do not designate as capital
gain dividends or that we retain as long-term capital gain. The
Jobs and Growth Tax Relief Reconciliation Act of 2003 reduced
the tax rate for qualified dividend income to 15%. However,
dividends from REITs generally are not subject to this lower
rate. REIT dividends paid to a U.S.&nbsp;stockholder that is a
corporation will not qualify for the dividends received
deduction generally available to corporations. As used herein,
